"""Week44 Days 3-4: estimator interfaces and evaluation boundaries."""

FIRST_SPLIT = '''from sklearn.datasets import load_iris
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
X, y = load_iris(return_X_y=True)
X_train, X_valid, y_train, y_valid = train_test_split(
    X, y, test_size=0.2, stratify=y, random_state=44)
'''

L(2, '1. Prepare Python and understand the estimator interface',
  'Install the practice tools separately and name the three main estimator operations.',
  [('Estimator API', 'A common object interface: construct with settings, fit on training examples, then use supported methods such as predict or transform. Not every estimator supports every method.'),
   ('Virtual environment; pip distribution', 'An isolated package environment keeps this lab separate from other projects. pip installs the distribution scikit-learn, while Python imports sklearn.'),
   ('return_X_y', 'A dataset-loader option that returns an input/target pair directly, rather than the richer named result object.')],
  'Use the commands below inside docs/python-week44. They call the new environment’s interpreter directly, so activation is optional. The initial package installation needs internet; the supplied dataset and examples work offline afterward. Reading the Study Hub page and its outputs never requires Python installation.\n\nAn estimator is an ordinary Python object with a consistent interface. Constructing it records settings. fit learns state from the supplied training data. predict produces answers if the estimator is a predictor. transform changes the representation if it is a transformer. For example, a scaler transforms measurements but does not classify a flower.\n\nThe first example checks the environment and reads shapes. return_X_y=True returns NumPy arrays here, so X.shape is (samples, features) and y.shape is one target per sample. Later examples request a DataFrame to retain names. Keep the distinction clear rather than assuming every loader call returns the same container type.',
  'import sklearn\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nX, y = load_iris(return_X_y=True)\nprint("Version:", sklearn.__version__)\nprint("X:", X.shape)\nprint("y:", y.shape)',
  'Load Iris as a DataFrame and check which table preserves feature names.',
  'from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nd = load_iris(as_frame=True)\nprint(type(d.data).__name__)\nprint(d.data.columns[0])',
  'Distinguish the three method roles in your own reference dictionary.',
  'roles = {"fit": "learn state from train", "predict": "produce target guesses", "transform": "change input representation"}\nfor method, role in roles.items():\n    print(method + ": " + role)',
  [('Which name belongs in pip install?', 'scikit-learn', 'sklearn.py from a random download', 'load_iris', 'The package distribution and import name differ.'),
   ('Does every estimator implement predict?', 'No; transformers may implement transform instead', 'Yes', 'Only if data is a list', 'The interface is consistent by role, not identical for every class.')],
  'Assume creating an estimator has already trained it.', 'Construct the object, fit it on training data, then call its supported inference method.',
  'No fitting occurs in this environment check. The array shapes establish the sample/feature convention before you train an object.',
  syntax='model = Estimator(setting=value)\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\npredictions = model.predict(X_new)',
  shell='python -m venv .venv\n.\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt\n.\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pytest -q\n# Run later examples with this environment’s Python.')

L(2, '2. Fit a nearest-neighbour classifier',
  'Explain how training examples and the neighbour count affect a simple classifier.',
  [('KNeighborsClassifier; k', 'A classifier that compares an input with stored training samples and combines the labels of its k nearest neighbours. n_neighbors chooses k; it is not learned automatically.'),
   ('Distance; feature scale', 'Distance measures separation between numeric feature vectors. Changing units can change which examples count as nearby.'),
   ('classes_; trailing underscore', 'classes_ records observed training labels after fitting. A trailing underscore convention commonly marks learned attributes.')],
  'A nearest-neighbour model is a concrete starting point. For a new four-measurement row, it finds nearby training measurements and uses their labels to choose a class. The default setting used here weights those selected neighbours uniformly. This is similarity-based prediction, not a botanical explanation.\n\nfit stores and prepares the training reference data. It does not search for the best neighbour count; we choose five before evaluation. This is also a reminder that not all learning algorithms fit a short equation or use iterative gradient updates.\n\nThe example first holds aside a fixed development partition. train_test_split splits paired X and y together; Day 4 explains the boundary and stratification in detail. Here the held-out rows are explicitly validation, not an untouched final test after repeated experiments. This first unscaled illustration helps explain the interface. Later we fit scaling inside a pipeline so inference uses the same learned transformation.',
  FIRST_SPLIT + 'from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nmodel = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5)\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\nprint("Neighbour count:", model.n_neighbors)\nprint("Known classes:", model.classes_.tolist())\nprint("Training shape:", X_train.shape)',
  'Construct a three-neighbour model and show that its setting exists before fit.',
  'from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nmodel = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=3)\nprint("Configured k:", model.n_neighbors)\nprint("Fitted classes available:", hasattr(model, "classes_"))',
  'Fit on the same training partition and inspect the number of input features.',
  FIRST_SPLIT + 'from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nmodel = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5).fit(X_train, y_train)\nprint("Learned input feature count:", model.n_features_in_)',
  [('Does fit automatically select the best k?', 'No; n_neighbors is a chosen setting', 'Always', 'Only on Iris', 'This estimator uses the configured count; selection is a separate procedure.'),
   ('What does classes_ describe?', 'Labels observed during fit', 'Every species on Earth', 'Only validation errors', 'Learned class vocabulary comes from the supplied training labels.')],
  'Call a constructor and immediately read learned classes_.', 'Fit the configured model on training examples before inspecting learned state.',
  'The common interface appears in order: split paired arrays, create an estimator, fit once, then inspect learned attributes. The neighbour count is configured state; classes_ and n_features_in_ are fitted state.')

L(2, '3. Predict a batch with the right shape',
  'Produce predictions without refitting and preserve the sample/feature boundary.',
  [('Batch prediction; two-dimensional input', 'predict expects rows of feature values. One four-feature sample has shape (1, 4), not a flat vector of shape (4,).'),
   ('NotFittedError', 'An explicit library error when an operation requires fitted state that the estimator does not yet have.')],
  'After fitting, predict accepts new input measurements without the target answers. It returns one label per input row. To interpret an integer label, use the dataset’s name mapping. Passing the true target into prediction defeats the purpose of the task.\n\nFor one sample, keep the row dimension: X_valid[:1] is a one-row table, while X_valid[0] is a flat array. This distinction comes from the NumPy indexing you studied earlier. An estimator cannot infer whether a flat sequence means several one-feature samples or one many-feature sample, so use the explicit two-dimensional convention.\n\nCalling fit again is not a harmless way to make prediction work. It replaces the fitted reference state using whichever rows you supply. Never refit on held-out labels just before reporting their score. The challenge catches the unfitted error deliberately and explains it, rather than suppressing unexpected failures.',
  FIRST_SPLIT + 'from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nmodel = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5).fit(X_train, y_train)\npredicted = model.predict(X_valid[:3])\nnames = load_iris().target_names\nprint("Batch shape:", X_valid[:3].shape)\nprint("Labels:", predicted.tolist())\nprint("Names:", [str(names[n]) for n in predicted])',
  'Predict exactly one validation row while retaining two dimensions.',
  FIRST_SPLIT + 'from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nm = KNeighborsClassifier().fit(X_train, y_train)\nprint("One-row shape:", X_valid[:1].shape)\nprint("Prediction count:", len(m.predict(X_valid[:1])))',
  'Catch and name the expected error from predicting before fit.',
  'from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nfrom sklearn.exceptions import NotFittedError\ntry:\n    KNeighborsClassifier().predict([[5.1, 3.5, 1.4, 0.2]])\nexcept NotFittedError:\n    print("Fit on training data before predict")',
  [('What is the shape of one sample with four inputs?', '(1, 4)', '(4,)', '(4, 1) always', 'Rows are samples, so keep a one-row two-dimensional table.'),
   ('Does predict need the correct target labels?', 'No', 'Yes; otherwise it cannot guess', 'Only for batches', 'Targets are used later for evaluation, not supplied as input answers.')],
  'model.fit(X_valid, y_valid) immediately before scoring validation.', 'Keep fitted training state and call model.predict(X_valid).',
  'The slice preserves a batch dimension. Mapping each predicted code to a name makes the output readable without changing what the estimator predicted.')

L(2, '4. Transform measurements using training statistics',
  'Distinguish fitting a transformer from applying its learned representation.',
  [('StandardScaler; mean_; scale_', 'StandardScaler learns each training column’s mean and population-standard-deviation scale, then applies (x - mean) / scale. It does not force a Gaussian distribution.'),
   ('fit_transform versus transform', 'fit_transform learns and applies on training data. transform reuses that learned state on validation or future rows without relearning.')],
  'Distance-based models are sensitive to input scale. A centimetre and a millimetre representation describe the same physical length but differ numerically by ten. If only one column changes units, it can dominate a distance calculation. Standardisation gives each nonconstant training feature a common reference scale.\n\nThe reference must come from training data. Fit the scaler there, then use that same state to transform all later rows. A held-out batch is not entitled to define its own centre and spread; that would give it a different coordinate system. StandardScaler uses population variance for its scale; a constant feature receives scale 1 rather than causing division by zero.\n\nThe tiny numeric table isolates this idea without a classifier. A future value can legitimately have a transformed value larger than one or outside the training range. Standardisation is not clipping or min-max normalisation. Day 5 connects imputation, scaling and prediction in one pipeline to avoid forgetting a step.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\ntrain = np.array([[10.0], [20.0], [30.0]])\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nscaled_train = scaler.fit_transform(train)\nprint("Training mean:", scaler.mean_.round(3).tolist())\nprint("Scaled train:", scaled_train.ravel().round(3).tolist())\nprint("Future 40:", scaler.transform([[40.0]]).ravel().round(3).tolist())',
  'Fit on [2,4,6] and show that transform on [100] leaves mean_ unchanged.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\ns = StandardScaler().fit([[2.0], [4.0], [6.0]])\nbefore = s.mean_.copy()\ns.transform([[100.0]])\nprint("Mean:", s.mean_.tolist())\nprint("Unchanged:", (s.mean_ == before).all())',
  'Check the transformed training mean is approximately zero.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nscaled = StandardScaler().fit_transform([[10.0], [20.0], [30.0]])\nprint("Training scaled mean:", round(float(scaled.mean()), 6))',
  [('Which method should reuse a fitted scaler for validation?', 'transform', 'fit_transform', 'fit on validation first', 'Validation must use the reference learned from training.'),
   ('Does StandardScaler guarantee every output lies between 0 and 1?', 'No', 'Yes', 'Only on integers', 'Standardisation uses mean and scale, not bounded min-max mapping.')],
  'Fit a separate scaler to every future prediction batch.', 'Fit on training data once and transform later rows with that same scaler.',
  'fit_transform both learns and transforms the training reference. transform uses the saved mean and scale for 40; it does not move the reference to the future value.')

L(2, '5. A complete first classifier in fewer than twenty lines',
  'Read an end-to-end example and account for every step.',
  [('accuracy_score', 'Compare paired true labels and predictions; the default result is the proportion correct. It does not fit or modify the model.'),
   ('Pipeline; make_pipeline; named_steps', 'A pipeline connects transformers and a final estimator. make_pipeline assigns step names automatically; named_steps exposes them for inspection. Fitting it learns preprocessing and the model on the supplied training rows.')],
  'Here is the complete small interface demonstration. Load packaged measurements, split paired rows, build a scaler plus five-neighbour classifier, fit on training and score predictions on validation. Every import supplies a visible operation. Read the code aloud before changing anything.\n\nThe pipeline fits StandardScaler on training rows, transforms them and fits the classifier on the transformed representation. During predict, it transforms new measurements with the stored scaler and asks the fitted classifier for labels. The same object therefore carries the inference sequence.\n\nThis fixed demonstration uses validation for learning the interface. Do not repeatedly try settings on these rows and then call their score an untouched final test. The project’s three-way split addresses that development issue. Also notice that a short example does not replace dataset understanding, a baseline or a limitation statement; those are required even when training takes only a few lines.',
  FIRST_SPLIT + 'from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\nmodel = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5))\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\npredicted = model.predict(X_valid)\nprint("Validation rows:", len(y_valid))\nprint("Accuracy:", round(accuracy_score(y_valid, predicted), 3))',
  'Compute the count of correct validation predictions with the same pipeline.',
  FIRST_SPLIT + 'from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nm = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5)).fit(X_train, y_train)\np = m.predict(X_valid)\nprint("Correct:", int((p == y_valid).sum()), "of", len(y_valid))',
  'Inspect the automatically named pipeline steps.',
  'from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nm = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5))\nprint(list(m.named_steps))',
  [('What does pipeline.predict do with preprocessing?', 'Reuses fitted transformations before predicting', 'Refits the scaler on the new batch', 'Skips all transformations', 'A fitted pipeline preserves the training transformation sequence.'),
   ('Does a short fit/predict script replace a baseline and scope statement?', 'No', 'Yes if accuracy is high', 'Only for Iris', 'Working code is only part of a defensible learning project.')],
  'Train on scaled inputs and predict on raw inputs outside the pipeline.', 'Keep transformation and prediction together in the same fitted pipeline.',
  'The first six lines load and split; the next imports define the pipeline components and metric. fit sees only training rows. Accuracy is computed afterward from paired validation labels and predictions.')

L(2, '6. Compare against DummyClassifier fairly',
  'Fit a no-feature baseline and compare counts on identical held-out rows.',
  [('DummyClassifier; most_frequent', 'A reference estimator that can ignore feature relationships and always predict the most common training class. Ties follow the estimator’s class ordering.'),
   ('Percentage points', 'The arithmetic difference between two percentages. It is not the same as percentage relative improvement.')],
  'A consistent estimator interface lets you fit a baseline with the same training labels and evaluate it with the same metric on the same validation rows. DummyClassifier(strategy="most_frequent") always chooses a training majority class. A balanced three-class training set gives an accuracy near one third on a similarly balanced evaluation set, regardless of impressive-looking feature columns.\n\nCompare correct counts as well as rounded accuracy. A one-case change in thirty cases moves accuracy by about 3.3 percentage points. This small denominator is a reason to avoid grand claims from tiny score differences. Do not compare one model on easy rows and another on a different partition.\n\nThe model’s advantage here means it uses measurement relationships more effectively than this trivial reference on these cases. It does not prove superiority over every classifier, a causal relationship, or performance in a new measurement environment. Keep the conclusion tied to the chosen baseline and evaluated sample.',
  FIRST_SPLIT + 'from sklearn.dummy import DummyClassifier\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\nmodels = {"majority": DummyClassifier(strategy="most_frequent"), "scaled KNN": make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5))}\nfor name, model in models.items():\n    model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n    p = model.predict(X_valid)\n    print(name, int((p == y_valid).sum()), "/", len(y_valid), f"accuracy={accuracy_score(y_valid, p):.3f}")',
  'Fit the majority baseline and show that it predicts only one class.',
  FIRST_SPLIT + 'from sklearn.dummy import DummyClassifier\nm = DummyClassifier(strategy="most_frequent").fit(X_train, y_train)\nprint("Predicted classes:", sorted(set(m.predict(X_valid).tolist())))',
  'Calculate the percentage-point change from 10/30 correct to 27/30 correct.',
  'baseline = 10 / 30\nmodel = 27 / 30\nprint("Percentage-point difference:", round(100 * (model - baseline), 1))',
  [('Where does most_frequent learn its guess?', 'Training labels', 'Final test labels', 'The largest feature value', 'The reference strategy must be fitted without evaluation answers.'),
   ('Why report correct counts as well as rounded accuracy?', 'They expose the evaluation denominator', 'They make the model causal', 'They remove all uncertainty', 'A small test can turn one changed prediction into a noticeable percentage change.')],
  'Select the majority class from validation labels before evaluating the baseline.', 'Fit the baseline on training labels and evaluate both methods on the same validation rows.',
  'Both entries receive the same X_train/y_train and X_valid/y_valid. The only intentional difference is their prediction method, so the comparison is interpretable.')

L(3, '1. Split paired rows together and check identities',
  'Construct disjoint partitions without scrambling the target alignment.',
  [('train_test_split', 'A helper that applies one split to all supplied aligned arrays. test_size controls the held-out proportion; random_state controls shuffling reproducibly.'),
   ('Index intersection; disjointness', 'Two partitions are disjoint when their row-identity intersection is empty. Row identities also help verify X and y stayed paired.')],
  'Imagine shuffling flower measurements and species labels independently. Their lengths remain equal, but the answers no longer belong to the measurements. A model can still run and produce meaningless results. Split X and y in the same call, or split stable row ids and use them consistently.\n\nThe helper returns X_train, X_holdout, y_train, y_holdout in that order. Holding out rows means the training call never sees those records. Check both the identity intersection and X/y index equality when using DataFrames and Series. Size checks alone cannot prove alignment.\n\nA row split is appropriate only under the stated assumption that rows are suitable independent evaluation cases. If repeated observations belong to the same person, device or source, disjoint row numbers can still hide related-source leakage. Part 6 discusses that limitation. Here the focus is the basic paired split operation.',
  'from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nd = load_iris(as_frame=True)\nXt, Xh, yt, yh = train_test_split(d.data, d.target, test_size=0.2, random_state=44)\nprint("Sizes:", len(Xt), len(Xh))\nprint("Overlap:", len(set(Xt.index) & set(Xh.index)))\nprint("Paired:", Xt.index.equals(yt.index), Xh.index.equals(yh.index))',
  'Check that the split covers every original Iris row exactly once.',
  'from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nd = load_iris(as_frame=True)\na, b = train_test_split(d.data, test_size=0.2, random_state=44)\nprint("All ids covered:", set(a.index) | set(b.index) == set(d.data.index))',
  'Show that independent reversal of labels breaks pairing despite equal lengths.',
  'ids_X = [0, 1, 2]\nids_y = [2, 1, 0]\nprint("Lengths match:", len(ids_X) == len(ids_y))\nprint("Row order matches:", ids_X == ids_y)',
  [('How should X and y be split?', 'Together using the same row selection', 'With unrelated shuffles', 'By sorting each column separately', 'Inputs and answers must keep their original correspondence.'),
   ('Do equal lengths alone prove pairing?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only on NumPy arrays', 'Ordering and row identity matter as well as size.')],
  'Shuffle the feature table and target series independently.', 'Pass both to one split operation and verify aligned identities.',
  'The empty intersection checks row overlap. Index equality checks pairing within each partition. These tests address different failure modes.')

L(3, '2. Stratify categories without claiming more than it does',
  'Preserve approximate label proportions and recognise small-class limits.',
  [('Stratification; stratify=y', 'A classification split can preserve approximate category proportions by sampling within labels. It does not guarantee representative features, groups or future conditions.'),
   ('Class support', 'The number of actual examples of a class in a partition. Very rare classes may make a requested stratified split impossible or uninformative.')],
  'A random split can leave a rare class absent from an evaluation subset. Stratification asks the splitter to preserve approximate target proportions. With fifty Iris rows per class and a twenty-percent holdout, this setup produces ten held-out rows of each class.\n\nStratification uses labels to assign evaluation membership; that is part of a planned splitting procedure, not permission to use held-out labels for feature fitting or model selection. It solves a proportion problem, not every evaluation problem. Two sources from the same device can still be related, and a future period can still differ.\n\nThe split requires enough examples and enough rows in each partition to allocate classes. A class with only one row cannot be represented independently on both sides. Do not silently duplicate that one row into train and test to make the function succeed. Gather more relevant data or redesign the evaluation and report the limitation.',
  'from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nd = load_iris(as_frame=True)\nXt, Xh, yt, yh = train_test_split(d.data, d.target, test_size=0.2, stratify=d.target, random_state=44)\nprint("Training class counts:", yt.value_counts().sort_index().to_dict())\nprint("Held-out class counts:", yh.value_counts().sort_index().to_dict())',
  'Compute expected counts for an exactly balanced 150-row dataset with a 20% holdout.',
  'per_class = 50\nheld_per_class = int(per_class * 0.2)\nprint("Held per class:", held_per_class)\nprint("Total held:", held_per_class * 3)',
  'Catch the error from a class with one example in a stratified split.',
  'from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\ntry:\n    train_test_split([[0], [1], [2], [3]], [0, 0, 0, 1], test_size=0.5, stratify=[0, 0, 0, 1], random_state=44)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Need enough independent examples per class")',
  [('What does stratification primarily preserve?', 'Approximate target proportions', 'Every feature distribution perfectly', 'Independence of related people', 'It controls label composition, not all dimensions of representativeness.'),
   ('Should a lone rare-class row be copied into both partitions?', 'No; that creates overlapping evidence', 'Yes', 'Only after changing its index', 'Renaming the same observation does not create an independent example.')],
  'Describe a stratified split as proof that all future populations are represented.', 'State which proportions were preserved and what assumptions remain.',
  'value_counts sorts the class ids for a stable report. The exact balance here follows this dataset and requested fraction; arbitrary datasets need not split into identical integer proportions.')

L(3, '3. Reserve training, validation and final test data',
  'Reproduce the lab’s 60/20/20 split and keep model choices away from the final test.',
  [('Development subset', 'The portion remaining after a final holdout is reserved. It can be divided into fitting and validation rows for development.'),
   ('Nested proportions', 'A second fraction applies to the remaining subset: 25% of 80% is 20% of the original data.')],
  'Reserve twenty percent for the final test first. From the remaining eighty percent, reserve one quarter for validation. The result is sixty percent training, twenty percent validation and twenty percent final test. Split X and y together and stratify each step.\n\nThe supplied make_splits helper implements exactly the code below. Its dictionary names each partition so a later call is less likely to confuse validation with test. Fixed indices make examples reproducible, but users who have already inspected the bundled final results should treat the exercise as a reproducible demonstration, not a new unseen scientific evaluation.\n\nFor this foundations project, k=5 is predeclared. If you experiment with a different k, compare on validation only. After choosing, explain whether you keep the original training fit or refit on the development set before final testing. Our lab keeps the original ninety-row training fit throughout so the boundary remains easy to audit.',
  'from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nd = load_iris(as_frame=True)\nXd, Xtest, yd, ytest = train_test_split(d.data, d.target, test_size=0.2, stratify=d.target, random_state=44)\nXtrain, Xvalid, ytrain, yvalid = train_test_split(Xd, yd, test_size=0.25, stratify=yd, random_state=44)\nprint("Train, validation, test:", len(Xtrain), len(Xvalid), len(Xtest))\nprint("Validation fraction:", len(Xvalid) / len(d.data))',
  'Use the helper and verify all three index sets are pairwise disjoint.',
  'from lab import make_splits\nd = make_splits()\na, b, c = [set(d["X_" + name].index) for name in ["train", "valid", "test"]]\nprint("Disjoint:", not (a & b or a & c or b & c))',
  'Explain numerically why a second 20% split would not give a 20% validation fraction.',
  'print("20% of remaining 80%:", round(0.8 * 0.2, 2))\nprint("25% of remaining 80%:", round(0.8 * 0.25, 2))',
  [('How much is 25% of the remaining 80%?', '20% of the original dataset', '25% of the original', '5% of the original', 'The second fraction applies to the development subset.'),
   ('Where should an exploratory comparison of k values happen?', 'Validation, keeping the final test reserved', 'Repeatedly on the final test', 'By choosing the best reported test in the lesson', 'Development and final reporting need separate roles.')],
  'Split off 20%, then 20% of the remainder and call the result 60/20/20.', 'Reserve 20%, then 25% of the remainder to obtain 60/20/20.',
  'The first call reserves thirty final rows; the second reserves thirty of the remaining120. No estimator or scaler has been fitted during these membership decisions.')

L(3, '4. Expose memorisation, underfitting and overfitting',
  'Compare training with validation and explain the bias-variance tradeoff cautiously.',
  [('DecisionTreeClassifier; max_depth', 'A tree learns feature-threshold branches from labelled training data. max_depth limits the number of branch levels and hence one aspect of model complexity.'),
   ('Underfitting; overfitting; bias-variance intuition', 'An overly rigid model may miss reusable patterns; an overly flexible model may follow training noise. Bias concerns systematic error; variance concerns sensitivity to the sampled training set. One score gap is a clue, not a formal decomposition.'),
   ('make_classification; score', 'make_classification locally generates synthetic labelled measurements for a controlled illustration. This classifier’s score(X, y) computes accuracy using the fitted model; it does not refit. It is not evidence about a real deployment population.')],
  'Training accuracy measures performance on examples used to learn the model. A sufficiently flexible tree can memorise many training labels, including noise. That is why perfect training accuracy is not the same as generalisation. In this illustration, synthetic noisy cases make that distinction easier to see than a very small clean benchmark.\n\nA shallow tree can be too rigid; an unrestricted one can chase details specific to the training sample. The bias-variance tradeoff describes this tension, not a rule that every deeper tree is worse. Compare validation errors, inspect the problem and collect appropriate evidence. A gap can also reflect distribution differences or data problems.\n\nThe chart shows training and validation scores for one fixed unrestricted tree. The challenge compares depths using validation as development evidence, without opening the final Iris project test. Do not select a final model from the chart alone or describe one random split as a universal ranking of algorithms.',
  'from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nX, y = make_classification(n_samples=240, n_features=8, n_informative=4, n_redundant=0, flip_y=0.2, random_state=44)\nXt, Xv, yt, yv = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3, stratify=y, random_state=44)\nm = DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state=44).fit(Xt, yt)\nscores = [m.score(Xt, yt), m.score(Xv, yv)]\nprint("Train/validation:", [round(s, 3) for s in scores])\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.6))\nax.bar(["Training", "Validation"], scores, color=["#376ea6", "#d27a32"])\nax.set(ylabel="Accuracy", ylim=(0, 1.08), title="Memorisation is not unseen performance")\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Train a one-neighbour classifier and compare training versus validation scores on the project split.',
  'from lab import make_splits\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nd = make_splits()\nm = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=1).fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nfor part in ["train", "valid"]:\n    print(part, round(m.score(d["X_" + part], d["y_" + part]), 3))',
  'Compare depths 1, 3 and unrestricted on the same synthetic development split; write why the best validation result is not a fresh final test.',
  'from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nX, y = make_classification(n_samples=240, n_features=8, n_informative=4, n_redundant=0, flip_y=0.2, random_state=44)\nXt, Xv, yt, yv = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3, stratify=y, random_state=44)\nfor depth in [1, 3, None]:\n    m = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=depth, random_state=44).fit(Xt, yt)\n    print("depth", depth, "train", round(m.score(Xt, yt), 3), "validation", round(m.score(Xv, yv), 3))',
  [('Does perfect training accuracy establish generalisation?', 'No; evaluate unseen cases', 'Yes', 'Only for a tree', 'The model can memorise cases it was trained on.'),
   ('Does one train/validation gap formally measure variance?', 'No; it is diagnostic evidence', 'Always', 'Only if plotted', 'Sensitivity to training samples and systematic error need more careful analysis.')],
  'Pick the most flexible model because its training score is perfect.', 'Assess development performance and inspect errors before freezing the workflow.',
  'The tree is fitted once on Xt/yt. score computes accuracy without refitting. Comparing its two bars exposes a difference between remembered training cases and held-out synthetic validation cases.',
  figure=('Training and validation accuracy for a flexible tree on synthetic noisy data', 'Both bars come from the executed example. The evaluation rows were held aside; a perfect training bar is not proof of future accuracy.'))

L(3, '5. Demonstrate leakage and repair the boundary',
  'Show how held-out values can enter preprocessing and remove that influence.',
  [('Data leakage; target leakage', 'Information unavailable in a real prediction enters model construction. It can come from future outcomes, held-out preparation statistics, related sources or repeated test-guided choices.'),
   ('Training-only statistic', 'A mean, median, vocabulary or selection rule learned only from the training partition, then reused unchanged elsewhere.'),
   ('SimpleImputer; statistics_', 'SimpleImputer learns a training-only replacement for missing inputs. strategy="median" selects the median and statistics_ stores it. transform applies replacements without learning from future values; Day 5 develops missingness policies.')],
  'Leakage is broader than accidentally including the target column. If a scaler learns its mean from training plus held-out rows, the held-out distribution has influenced the workflow. Imputation medians, category vocabularies and supervised feature selection can cross the same boundary. A pipeline helps enforce the order, but cannot repair future-information columns you already supplied.\n\nThe small numeric example deliberately includes a held-out extreme. The incorrect all-data mean is much larger than the training-only mean. This proves contamination of fitted state; it does not claim that leakage must improve every score on every dataset. The methodological problem exists even when the measured score happens to fall.\n\nThe repair is to split first, fit the transformer on training values, and transform held-out values with the saved state. The challenge gives an even more obvious target leak: copying the answer into the inputs yields an apparently perfect lookup without building a useful predictor. Explain why such a result is unusable at prediction time.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\ntrain = np.array([[1.0], [2.0], [3.0]])\nheld = np.array([[100.0]])\nwrong = StandardScaler().fit(np.vstack([train, held]))\nright = StandardScaler().fit(train)\nprint("Leaked mean:", wrong.mean_.tolist())\nprint("Training-only mean:", right.mean_.tolist())\nprint("Held-out transformed:", right.transform(held).ravel().round(3).tolist())',
  'Fit a median imputer on [1,3,NaN] and show an extreme held-out value cannot change its median.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\ni = SimpleImputer(strategy="median").fit([[1.0], [3.0], [np.nan]])\ni.transform([[1000.0], [np.nan]])\nprint("Training median:", i.statistics_.tolist())',
  'Demonstrate the meaningless perfect score from copying labels into predictions, then name the problem.',
  'actual = [0, 1, 2, 1]\nleaked_predictions = actual.copy()\nprint("Apparent accuracy:", sum(a == p for a, p in zip(actual, leaked_predictions)) / len(actual))\nprint("Invalid: answers are unavailable when predicting")',
  [('Can unsupervised preprocessing leak held-out information?', 'Yes; fitted statistics can include held-out values', 'No because labels are absent', 'Only in a neural network', 'The boundary includes input distribution information as well as target answers.'),
   ('Must leakage improve every particular reported score?', 'No; contamination is still invalid', 'Always', 'Only for scaling', 'A methodological violation is not defined by whether one score increased.')],
  'Fit the scaler on all rows before making a supposedly held-out split.', 'Split first; fit on training and only transform held-out data.',
  'The held-out100 shifts the incorrect mean. The correct transformer learns only1,2,3; transform uses that reference without modifying it.')

L(3, '6. Match the split to people, sources and time',
  'Identify when random stratified rows are the wrong evaluation design.',
  [('GroupShuffleSplit; group leakage', 'A group-aware splitter keeps each supplied source group entirely on one side. group ids represent related observations, not prediction targets.'),
   ('Temporal split', 'Train on an earlier period and evaluate on a later period when the real task predicts the future. Features must also respect their own availability time.')],
  'Suppose each person has several measurements. Random row splitting can put one person’s records in both training and validation. The task may then measure recognition of familiar people rather than performance on new people. Split by person or source id when independence is defined at that level.\n\nFor future-demand prediction, shuffling tomorrow’s rows into training can make the evaluation unrealistic. Use an appropriate chronological boundary, and ensure rolling aggregates or labels do not carry future information backward. Ordered rows alone do not repair a feature computed from the whole timeline. Advanced time-series and grouped cross-validation arrive later; these simple boundaries establish the idea.\n\nThe worked example uses six fictional source groups and checks that no group crosses the split. It does not claim the label proportions are stratified or that every real dataset has enough groups. Choose the split according to intended use, label support and data collection. A familiar function default is not a substitute for that reasoning.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GroupShuffleSplit\nX = np.arange(12).reshape(-1, 1)\ngroups = np.repeat(np.arange(6), 2)\nsplitter = GroupShuffleSplit(n_splits=1, test_size=0.33, random_state=44)\ntrain, held = next(splitter.split(X, groups=groups))\nprint("Group overlap:", sorted(set(groups[train]) & set(groups[held])))\nprint("Rows:", len(train), len(held))',
  'Split an ordered eight-day series into six earlier training days and two later evaluation days.',
  'days = list(range(1, 9))\ntrain, held = days[:6], days[6:]\nprint("Train:", train)\nprint("Later:", held)\nprint("Ordered boundary:", max(train) < min(held))',
  'Choose the evaluation design for new-person prediction and next-month prediction.',
  'designs = {"new person": "group by person id", "next month": "chronological holdout"}\nfor task, design in designs.items():\n    print(task + ": " + design)',
  [('Do disjoint row ids guarantee no related-source leakage?', 'No; related rows can have different ids', 'Yes', 'Only when stratified', 'The independence unit may be a person, source or time block.'),
   ('What split better matches next-month prediction?', 'Earlier training and later evaluation', 'Randomly mixing all future and past rows', 'Training on the next month first', 'The evaluation should reflect information available before future prediction.')],
  'Use random stratified rows for every task regardless of repeated sources or time.', 'Choose the independence unit and time boundary before selecting a splitting method.',
  'GroupShuffleSplit returns positional indices. The intersection compares source ids, not merely row ids. The temporal exercise separately checks that every training day precedes evaluation.')
